# The test harness and the hook are read by bash and by Python on every
# platform, including Windows, where the suite runs under Git Bash. A CRLF
# checkout breaks bash outright, so the working tree stays LF-only everywhere.
* text=auto eol=lf

# The launcher is the one exception, and it is not a preference. It is a
# polyglot: cmd.exe reads the batch half and /bin/sh reads the POSIX tail, and
# the two want different line endings. Measured 2026-08-25 on GitHub runners,
# four arms driven end to end -- LF-only makes cmd.exe lose its file position
# across a parenthesized block, so the goto after it dies with `The system
# cannot find the batch label specified` and the launcher exits 1 with empty
# stdout, which is not a deny and lets the tool call through unscanned; CRLF
# throughout kills the POSIX half instead, on `set: Illegal option -`.
#
# So the file carries CRLF down to the CMDBLOCK terminator and LF after it.
# cmd.exe never reads past the terminator and sh discards everything above it
# as heredoc content, so each half gets what it needs. `-text` is what stops
# git normalising that away in either direction; `make launcher` asserts both
# the endings and this attribute, because a rule this easy to undo cannot live
# in a comment.
hooks/run-spill-guard.cmd -text

# The UTF-16 corpus fixture is the encoding under test, so nothing may
# re-encode it. `text=auto` above already leaves a NUL-bearing file alone, and
# that is a detection rather than a rule -- this is the rule.
*-utf16le.* -text

# The failing-launcher fixture is a byte-exact artifact from a measurement on a
# Windows runner, and uniform LF is the property under test, so nothing may
# normalise it. Its sha256 is asserted by the control that drives it.
testdata/launcher/*.cmd -text
